# Normalize to LF in the repository; Git still checks out native line endings on Windows.
# Keeps `cargo fmt --check` and diffs consistent across the Linux/macOS/Windows CI matrix.
* text=auto

*.rs    text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.sql   text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf

# Shell scripts must keep LF wherever they are checked out. `sh` on Linux and macOS treats a
# trailing CR as part of the interpreter path or the command, so a CRLF checkout produces
# "bad interpreter: No such file or directory" -- which is what would have happened to
# ports/machine/android/gradlew and the build scripts beside it.
*.sh      text eol=lf
gradlew   text eol=lf
*.gradle  text eol=lf
*.java    text eol=lf
*.xml     text eol=lf
*.pro     text eol=lf
*.properties text eol=lf

# Data a shell script parses, meaning crates/machine/km-banks/data/soundfont-banks.conf --
# the bank table, read by `tools/setup/soundfont-banks.sh` and through it by four other scripts.
# **This is a fix, not consistency.** On a Windows checkout the table came out CRLF and the shell
# reader found no bank at all: `tools/dev/soundfont.sh --list` printed a header and no rows,
# `soundfont-measure.sh` refused every bank by name, and `tools/setup/fetch-assets.sh` could not find
# the bundled bank to download -- which fails `task dist:bin`.
#
# **Where the CR actually bites is worth being exact about**, because the obvious suspect is
# innocent. `KM_BANKS` is built with `sed`, and Git Bash's sed reads in text mode and strips the CR,
# so that list is clean. It is `km_bank`'s `while IFS= read -r line` that keeps it: `${line%]}` then
# leaves the id as `generaluser<CR>`, no block ever matches, and the function returns 2 for every
# name. Verified rather than reasoned: `printf 'A\r\n'` through `read -r` yields `A^M` where
# `sed -n 1p` yields `A`.
#
# That reader now strips the CR itself as well, so the two halves of the table agree about a stray
# one the way the Rust half always did with `trim_end()`. This pin is still the door that matters --
# a file arriving with CRLF is a bug wherever it is tolerated -- but "one definition, two readers"
# ought to mean two readers that behave alike.
#
# Worth recording how it got past its own check: the refactor that split this file out verified that
# `soundfont.sh --list` and `soundfont-debug.sh --list` produce *byte-identical* output before and
# after, and two empty tables are byte-identical. A check that compares two outputs cannot see that
# both are wrong.
#
# **And this pin does not repair a copy already checked out.** A checkout made before it stays CRLF
# until the file is re-materialised: `git rm --cached <file> && git checkout -- <file>`, or a
# tree-wide `git add --renormalize .`. Merging the pin and re-running the failing command without
# that step reproduces the failure exactly and reads as "the fix did not work".
*.conf    text eol=lf

# The prose checker's two data files. `check-prose.sh` hands `prose-sentences.awk` to `awk -f`, and
# it matches a path in `prose-converted.txt` with `grep -xF`. A CR ends every line of the program and
# every path in the list, so both stay LF wherever they are checked out. The other `.txt` files here
# are UltraStar fixtures, which carry whatever bytes the case under test needs.
*.awk     text eol=lf
tools/dev/prose-converted.txt text eol=lf

# Linker version scripts, meaning tools/port/libc_n.map, which `.cargo/config.toml` passes as
# `--version-script`. Unlike the shell scripts above this is *not* load-bearing: lld was tested with
# a CRLF copy and linked it without complaint, so this is consistency rather than a fix. Pinned
# anyway, so the linker gets the same bytes on every platform and nobody has to re-establish that.
*.map     text eol=lf

# ...and the Windows companion keeps CRLF, for the same reason in reverse: cmd.exe needs it.
gradlew.bat text eol=crlf
*.jar     binary

# The Linux desktop entry, meaning crates/machine/karaokemachine/linux/karaokemachine.desktop. Same
# reasoning as the Debian maintainer scripts below, and the same delivery path: `tools/platform/linux/deb.sh`
# mounts this working copy into the build container, so a CRLF checkout is what would go *into* the
# package. The desktop entry spec separates lines with LF only, so a trailing CR becomes part of
# the value -- `Exec=karaokemachine\r` and `Icon=karaokemachine\r` both then resolve to nothing, and
# the failure shows up as an installed application with no icon that will not launch from the menu.
*.desktop text eol=lf

# Systemd units, meaning crates/machine/karaokemachine/linux/karaokemachine.service. Exactly the desktop
# entry's problem one directory over, and it arrives by the same route: `tools/platform/linux/deb.sh`
# packages this working copy, so a CRLF checkout is what lands in /lib/systemd/system on the
# appliance. Systemd does not strip a trailing CR, so `ExecStart=/usr/bin/karaokemachine\r` becomes
# a request to run a binary whose path ends in a carriage return -- and the unit fails to start with
# a message naming a path that looks perfectly correct in every terminal that does not show control
# characters.
*.service text eol=lf

# The boot splash, meaning crates/machine/karaokemachine/linux/plymouth/. The third file in that one
# directory to need this line and the one with the least forgiving reader: the theme is packaged from
# this working copy, unpacked into the *initramfs*, and parsed before the root filesystem is mounted.
# A trailing CR makes `ImageDir=/usr/share/plymouth/themes/karaokemachine\r` a directory that does
# not exist, so `Image("logo.png")` finds nothing and the splash is a bare ground with no mark on it
# -- with no console, no journal and no way to ask, because none of that exists yet.
*.plymouth text eol=lf
*.script   text eol=lf

# Property lists, meaning tools/platform/macos/Info.plist. Not load-bearing -- XML normalizes CRLF to LF
# during parsing, so macOS reads it either way -- but the `*.xml` rule above misses the `.plist`
# extension, and there is no reason for the two to disagree.
*.plist   text eol=lf

# Binary assets must never be transformed.
*.sf2   binary
*.mid   binary
*.kar   binary
*.kmpkg binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.png   binary
# The Windows and macOS icon containers. Content detection would classify these as binary anyway;
# named for the same reason as the formats above, so the rule is a statement rather than a bet on
# heuristics.
*.ico   binary
*.icns  binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.webp  binary
*.wav   binary

# Debian maintainer scripts, meaning crates/machine/karaokemachine/debian/{postinst,postrm}. They have no
# extension, so the *.sh rule above misses them -- and they are the one kind of script that runs on
# somebody else's machine, at `dpkg --configure` time, where "#!/bin/sh\r" fails exactly as
# described above. A CRLF checkout here ships a broken package rather than breaking the build that
# made it.
crates/machine/karaokemachine/debian/* text eol=lf
# **And the tools package's, which are the same files under a second name.** A path rule protects a
# path and nothing else, so a second package's scripts are a second line -- the failure it prevents
# is silent on the machine that commits them and fatal on the one that installs them.
tools/cmd/km-package-builder/debian-tools/* text eol=lf

# Dockerfiles. A trailing CR turns a line-continuation backslash into an argument, so the long
# `RUN apt-get install … \` blocks in tools/platform/linux/Dockerfile fail on a CRLF checkout.
Dockerfile   text eol=lf
*.dockerfile text eol=lf

# The SoundFont's license, which `tools/setup/fetch-assets.sh` downloads from GeneralUser GS's own
# repository and copies in beside the bank. **Upstream ships it CRLF**, and this is the one tracked
# file in the tree whose contents arrive from a fetch rather than from an editor -- so the working
# copy has to be whatever the fetch writes, or the two disagree forever.
#
# Under the `* text=auto` rule at the top they did disagree, on every platform except the one this is
# mostly developed on. Windows checks out CRLF, so the fetched file matched and nothing looked wrong;
# macOS and Linux check out LF, so `git status` reported the file modified after every
# `tools/setup/fetch-assets.sh`, and the script's own `cmp -s` guard never matched either -- it re-copied
# and printed "installed" on every single run, where the bank beside it printed "current".
#
# `eol=crlf` pins the *working tree* form on all three, which is what the fetch produces. The blob in
# the repository stays LF, normalized as everything else is, so no history changes and nothing else
# has to know.
assets/soundfont/LICENSE.txt text eol=crlf
